        "## Part 1: Data + Identification Strategy\n",
        "\n",
        "**Research question:** Does participation in a job-training program cause higher post-program earnings?\n",
        "\n",
        "**Dataset:** Lalonde / Dehejia-Wahba job training data. I use NSW treated observations and CPS comparison controls, which creates an observational setting with more than 1,000 observations.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Treatment:** `treat`, equal to 1 if the person participated in NSW job training.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Outcome:** `re78`, real earnings in 1978.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Controls:** age, education, race indicators, marital status, degree status, 1974 earnings, 1975 earnings, and unemployment indicators.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Identification strategy:** Double Machine Learning under conditional independence.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Key assumption:** After controlling for observed pre-treatment characteristics, treatment assignment is as good as random.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why prediction alone is insufficient:** A predictive model can estimate which people have higher earnings, but it cannot isolate the counterfactual effect of training participation. The policy question is not only “who earns more?” but “what happens to earnings if training participation changes?”"
      ]
    },
