      "source": [
        "## Part 9: Threats to Identification Draft\n",
        "\n",
        "**Most serious threat:** Selection on unobservables. DML adjusts for observed characteristics such as age, education, race, marital status, degree status, and pre-treatment earnings, but it cannot adjust for unobserved motivation, local labor-market opportunities, caseworker screening, or health constraints.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Direction of bias:** The direction is ambiguous. If more motivated individuals selected into training, the estimate may be inflated. If the program targeted especially disadvantaged workers, the estimate may be deflated.\n",
        "\n",
        "**What would address it:** The strongest solution would be randomized assignment or a valid instrument that affects training participation but does not directly affect later earnings. Richer pre-treatment work histories, local unemployment rates, and program eligibility rules would also help.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Interpretation:** The estimate should be interpreted as a credible observational causal estimate under conditional independence, not as an experimental gold-standard effect."
      ]
    },
